### Summary

`Sql.Expr<T>(FormattableString)` with an interpolation whose values are all client-evaluable is registered for by-value query-cache comparison, but the registered `FormattableString` is compared by reference. A fresh instance is produced on every evaluation, so the comparison never matches and the query is rebuilt on every execution.

### Background

`FormattableString` is an abstract class implementing only `IFormattable` — it overrides neither `Equals` nor `GetHashCode`, and does not implement `IEnumerable`. An interpolated string reaches the expression tree as a `FormattableStringFactory.Create(format, new object[]{ … })` call, so every evaluation allocates a new instance.

The sibling overload `Sql.Expr<T>(RawSqlString, params object[])` is unaffected: its format argument carries `[SqlQueryDependent]`, and its `object[]` is translated element-wise, so neither reaches the by-value channel.

### Current behavior

- `Source/LinqToDB/Sql/Sql.Expressions.cs:563` — `Expr<T>(FormattableString sql)` declares no `[SqlQueryDependent]` on `sql`, unlike the `RawSqlString` overload at `:569`.
- `Source/LinqToDB/Sql/Sql.Expressions.cs:537`–`:543` — `ExprBuilder` decomposes the argument through `PrepareRawSqlArguments` and converts the unpacked *elements*; the `FormattableStringFactory.Create` node itself is never handed to the converter.
- `Source/LinqToDB/Internal/Linq/Builder/ExpressionBuilder.QueryBuilder.cs:389` — `RegisterExtensionAccessors` therefore sees an untranslated, client-evaluable argument and calls `ParametersContext.MarkAsValue` with the evaluated `FormattableString`.
- `Source/LinqToDB/Internal/Linq/Query.cs:101` — the registered value is compared with `value1.Equals(value2)`, which is reference equality for `FormattableString`, so two evaluations never match.

Reproduced on `SQLite.Classic`:

```csharp
[Test, QueryCacheTest]
public void InterpolatedExprIsCached(
    [IncludeDataSources(ProviderName.SQLiteClassic)] string context,
    [Values(1, 2)]                                   int    iteration)
{
    using var db    = GetDataContext(context);
    using var table = db.CreateLocalTable<SomeTable>();

    var value = iteration;

    var query     = table.Select(_ => Sql.Expr<int>($"{value}"));
    var cacheMiss = query.GetCacheMissCount();

    query.First().ShouldBe(iteration);

    if (iteration == 2)
        query.GetCacheMissCount().ShouldBe(cacheMiss);
}
```

Iteration 2 fails with `query.GetCacheMissCount() should be 1L but was 2L`. The same test against `Sql.Expr<int>("{0}", value)` — identical format, identical value, only the overload differs — passes.

### Expected behavior

An interpolated fragment with equal format and equal argument values should reuse the cached query. Comparing a `FormattableString` by `Format` plus element-wise `GetArguments()` would achieve that, mirroring the `RawSqlString` case that `Source/LinqToDB/Mapping/SqlQueryDependentAttribute.cs:35`–`:36` already carries.

### Notes

Only a **fully client-evaluable** interpolation is affected. When the interpolation references a column the `Create` call is not client-evaluable, registration is skipped, and the node stays in the main expression where it is compared structurally.

Pre-existing, not caused by #5841 — surfaced while reviewing it. The master lines that matter are unchanged: `Query.cs:101` is `value1.Equals(value2)` there too, `Sql.Expressions.cs:563` carries no attribute, and `ExpressionCacheHelpers.cs:14` keeps `FormattableString` out of the strip-from-key set. #5841 routes this comparison through its new `ExpressionEqualityComparer.CompareValues`, which makes the fix a single-place change without altering the outcome — `FormattableString` matches none of its branches and falls through to `Equals`.

Related: #5843 covers the same theme on a different path (constants that remain in the cache key, compared by `EqualsToVisitor`).

The reproduction above was run end to end; the master-side claim rests on reading the three lines cited, not on a separate run.

