## Summary

When an entity has a `HasQueryFilter` and is projected and `LeftJoin`-ed, a `joined != null ? joined.X : fallback`
conditional is discarded in every operand of a `Concat`/`Union` **except the first**. Only the `.X` branch survives,
so a non-matching left join yields `NULL` instead of the fallback.

Remove the query filter and the same query is translated correctly. It is positional — swapping the operands moves
the breakage to the other side.

**Regression:** introduced in **6.4.0**. Correct in 6.0.0, 6.1.0, 6.2.0, 6.2.1 and 6.3.0.

Originally caught against SQL Server in CI; the SQLite reduction below is the same shape, so this is not
provider-specific. Repro run on `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite` 9.0.5, .NET 10.

## Repro

```csharp
[Table("Orders")]
public class Order
{
    [Column(IsPrimaryKey = true)] public int Id { get; set; }
    [Column] public string CustomerCode { get; set; } = null!;
}

[Table("Customers")]
public class Customer
{
    [Column(CanBeNull = false, IsPrimaryKey = true)] public string Code { get; set; } = null!;
    [Column] public string Name { get; set; } = null!;
}
```

```csharp
var ms = new MappingSchema();
var b = new FluentMappingBuilder(ms);
var off = false;                       // not a compile-time constant, so the filter is not folded away
b.Entity<Customer>().HasQueryFilter((e, ctx) => off || e.Name != "");
b.Build();

using var db = new DataConnection(new DataOptions().UseSQLite(cs).UseMappingSchema(ms));
db.CreateTable<Order>();
db.CreateTable<Customer>();
db.Insert(new Customer { Code = "C1", Name = "Acme" });
db.Insert(new Order { Id = 1, CustomerCode = "C1" });        // resolves
db.Insert(new Order { Id = 2, CustomerCode = "MISSING" });   // does not

IQueryable<string> Names(int id) =>
    from o in db.GetTable<Order>().Where(o => o.Id == id)
    from c in db.GetTable<Customer>()
        .Select(x => new { x.Code, Name = x.Name + "!" })
        .LeftJoin(x => x.Code == o.CustomerCode)
    select c != null ? c.Name : "Unknown";

Names(1).Concat(Names(2)).ToList();
```

Three ingredients are each necessary — drop any one and it does not reproduce:

- the `HasQueryFilter` on the joined entity;
- a filter predicate that is not a compile-time constant (`(e, ctx) => true` is elided);
- a **computed** projected column (`x.Name + "!"`); with a plain column the join is flattened.

## Expected

`["Acme!", "Unknown"]`, and both operands keep the `CASE` (this is 6.3.0, and 6.4.0 with the filter removed):

```sql
SELECT CASE WHEN [c_2].[Code] IS NOT NULL THEN [c_2].[Name] ELSE 'Unknown' END
FROM [Orders] [o]
    LEFT JOIN ( SELECT [c_1].[Code], Coalesce([c_1].[Name], '') || '!' as [Name] FROM [Customers] [c_1]
                WHERE [c_1].[Name] <> '' OR [c_1].[Name] IS NULL ) [c_2] ON [c_2].[Code] = [o].[CustomerCode]
WHERE [o].[Id] = @id
UNION ALL
SELECT CASE WHEN [c_4].[Code] IS NOT NULL THEN [c_4].[Name] ELSE 'Unknown' END
FROM [Orders] [o_1]
    LEFT JOIN ( ... ) [c_4] ON [c_4].[Code] = [o_1].[CustomerCode]
WHERE [o_1].[Id] = @id_1
```

## Actual (6.4.0)

`["Acme!", NULL]` — the second operand's projection is reduced to the bare column:

```sql
UNION ALL
SELECT
	[c_4].[Name]          -- CASE ... ELSE 'Unknown' dropped
FROM [Orders] [o_1]
    LEFT JOIN ( ... ) [c_4] ON [c_4].[Code] = [o_1].[CustomerCode]
WHERE [o_1].[Id] = @id_1
```

## Notes

- With a longer fallback chain (`a != null ? a.X : b != null ? b.X : "Unknown"`) the whole chain collapses to
  `a.X`; `b` stays in the `FROM` clause but is never referenced in the `SELECT`.
- The result is a `NULL` in a column the projection type declares non-nullable, so it surfaces as bad data rather
  than an exception.


